36:- P-39 **Every batch's gate measures the checker count, and its total never turns the gate red.** The checker count and the distance to the switch-over both count the compiled checker's errors on the interpreter's library. The count stops where the checker crashes or meets an error that it cannot get past; the distance counts every error, over all apis and components (F-53 defines both and holds their values). A rise of the count's total is reported and never fails a batch on its own. The count's stage, step 8 of the batch's gate (`explorations/coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.md`), can still turn the gate red on the faults that F-66 names. One is a checker crash that no rung of the batch listed in advance as expected, in the field `expectedCheckerCrash` of its manifest. The gates of phase 3's batches, whose work drives the distance down, also measure the distance, in a stage that only reports. Why: the distance's full measurement takes far longer than the count, and a batch pays for it only when the distance is the number that it tries to move.
