28:- F-35 **The `FortressLibrary` api has 8 declarations with a dead size, a size parameter that occurs in no parameter type, return type or body.** They check clean, and they go only by a diff shown to the curator; the 17 vector and matrix operators' dead sizes went by decision E's diff (P-40; F-264). They stand in the oldest library in the history, of 2007-01-04 (`72ae6881b`), where the six vector forms, scalar ones included, carry the matrix product's `nat n, nat m, nat p` beside it, with no `where` clause; in 2008 Jan-Willem Maessen took them out of the component while fixing walk's unification ("tons of latent type bugs came out", `32153798d`), and the api kept them. Where the component omits a dead size, the export checker reports the api's declaration missing. Detail: `perf-probes/nat/REPORT.md` sections 2 and 7.
71:- F-78 **The compiled checker instantiates a type parameter that nothing at a call fixes at the intersection of its upper bounds, never `BottomType`, and tries a call by subtyping without the expected type first.** It builds P-48 and P-49. An argument of intersection type fixes a parameter at the whole intersection, not at one conjunct (row 541). The run time has no class for an intersection type, so such an instance dies as it loads (row 559). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferResultOnly*` and `InferContextKeepsFit`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-instance-bound/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 7.
72:- F-79 **In the interpreter's library, `IntLiteral`, the type that the compiled checker gives an integer numeral, is declared on the model of the compiler's prelude: a sibling of the number types under `Number`, which each integer type, `QQ`, `RR64` and `AnyIntegral` convert from by coercion.** It builds the library half of P-76. Under walk a numeral is still a `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ`, so no numeral reaches these declarations until walk's half is built (F-74). `IntLiteral` declares itself the members of `Integral[\I\]` that a numeral could not reach, among them the getters `zero` and `one`, `odd`, `even`, `floor` and `DIVIDES`. There are three reasons: for some, no declaration of `Integral` applies to a sibling type even with coercion; for others, the declarations that apply by coercion tie; and a getter must be the type's own member. Each integer type declares its own comparisons, `CMP`, `MAXNUM` and `MINNUM`, and a `^` with an `IntLiteral` exponent. Without them, a typed integer with a numeral operand ties between a declaration inherited from a generic trait and the same operator's own declarations in `ZZ64`, `QQ` and `RR64`. The compiled checker refuses `(3).minimum` and `(3).maximum`, `ZZ32`'s getters (row 581). Gated under walk by `tests/IntegerOrderNumerals.fss`, which checks each integer type's own comparisons, `MAXNUM` and `MINNUM`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-library/REPORT.md` sections 4 and 6.
89:- F-221 **The compiled checker retries a call written as another call's argument against each enclosing declaration's parameter type, where the enclosing call is not accepted otherwise.** It builds P-51 under P-49, so a declaration that accepts the call as written is never passed over. A reduction's plain-bounded big operator, an argument of `__bigOperator` once desugared, so takes the reduction's expected type; an F-bounded one is still refused (row 731). A chain `a OPLUS b OPLUS c` that only the retry makes acceptable to a multifix declaration now runs it, not the binary one. Walk gives such an argument no expected type (row 705). Gated by `compiler_tests/InferCallArgument*` and `InferBigOperatorExpectedType`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-argument-context/REPORT.md` sections 1, 5 and 8, and `SKEPTIC.md` finding 5.
158:- F-143 **Under `walk`, a type parameter whose bound mentions itself and that nothing at a call fixes is left open.** The open type is below every type, and every value matches it. So a reduction that writes no static argument runs on its elements' types. A tuple, arrow or generic type that holds the open type admits no more than at `BottomType`, so `(Int, Int)` is not below `(OPEN, OPEN)` (row 663, contested). A declaration whose parameter type is the open type is the most specific, so it is chosen for every argument. Gated by `tests/UnwrittenReductionWalk.fss`. Rows 645, 646 and 662 are fixed by the declared type's and the elements' instance (F-228); an empty reduction that no declared type directs still takes `ZZ32`'s identity (row 716). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-open-param/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 11.
